 ### Isosurface Scene Representation
-ECoNGS represents isosurface scenes by extracting triangular meshes at different isovalues over a value range (e.g., [-1, 1]) to form basic scenes, then training them jointly [S2, unofficial]. For a composed isosurface scene with 53.9M triangles, ECoNGS achieves a model size of 1.57 MB with 331 FPS, compared to the triangular mesh's 1002.9 MB and 101 FPS [S2, unofficial]. Compared to direct triangular mesh extraction, ECoNGS achieves high fidelity with much smaller model size (e.g., 0.65 MB vs 317.5 MB for iso=-0.5) and higher FPS (468 vs 101), but has a slightly higher GPU/host memory footprint during rendering (3.30/7.61 GB vs 0.31/1.86 GB). Memory cost stays constant regardless of scene complexity [S2, unofficial].
+ECoNGS represents isosurface scenes by extracting triangular meshes at different isovalues over a value range (e.g., $[-1, 1]$) to form basic scenes, then training them jointly [S3, unofficial]. For a composed isosurface scene with 53.9M triangles, ECoNGS achieves a model size of 1.57 MB with 331 FPS, compared to the direct triangular mesh's 1002.9 MB and 101 FPS [S3, unofficial]. Compared to direct triangular mesh extraction, ECoNGS achieves high fidelity with a much smaller model size (e.g., 0.65 MB vs 317.5 MB for iso=-0.5) and higher FPS (468 vs 101), but incurs a slightly higher GPU/host memory footprint during rendering (3.30/7.61 GB vs 0.31/1.86 GB) [S3, unofficial]. The memory cost remains constant regardless of scene complexity [S3, unofficial].
 
 ### CPU-based Software Rasterizer with Orbital Camera
-A standalone CPU-based rendering engine that performs barycentric triangle rasterization, depth buffering (Z-buffer), and directional Lambertian shading, featuring an interactive orbital camera controlled by spherical coordinates [S3, unofficial]. The steps include performing barycentric triangle rasterization to determine which pixels are covered by each triangle, applying depth buffering to handle visibility and occlusion, and applying directional Lambertian shading to compute lighting based on surface normals and a light direction [S3, unofficial].
